{
  "0": "For all four datasets, strengthen the existing training-frequency history feature: change its inverse-log-frequency exponent from -0.25 to -0.5, retaining mean-one normalization, clipping to [0.75,1.25], unseen-item scale 1 and padding scale 0. The previous feature improved validation; stronger attenuation may further reduce common-item dominance. Compute counts only from training interactions and checkpoint the scales for identical inference. Keep embeddings, HSTU topology, readout, loss and optimizer fixed.",
  "1": "For all four datasets, jointly lower learning rate from 1e-3 to 5e-4 and dropout from 0.3 to 0.2. Smaller updates may stabilize pairwise optimization, while reduced dropout preserves more signal in predominantly short histories and offsets slower fitting. Retain width 64, two blocks, two heads, batch size 128, maxlen 128, AdamW and existing weight decay. Keep features, parameterization, interaction/readout formulas and loss unchanged; retain seed 42, 200 batches per epoch and the 200-epoch cap.",
  "2": "For all four datasets, replace the batch-wide valid-position loss average with a per-history average: compute each sampled history's mean softplus(-(positive_score-negative_score)) over valid targets, then average those means across the batch. This gives sampled users equal objective weight instead of letting longer histories dominate, potentially improving user-averaged HR@10. Preserve sampled pairs, features, embeddings, HSTU topology, scoring/readout, optimizer, scheduler and training settings.",
  "3": "For all four datasets, shorten the causal recency-pooling decay scale from 4 to 2 while retaining readout_t=0.75*h_t+0.25*p_t. Define p_t using normalized exp(-(t-j)/2) weights over nonpadding states j<=t. This preserves the context contribution that outperformed a weaker pooling weight while concentrating it on immediate interests. Keep truncation, cumulative observed-position IDs, input features, embeddings, HSTU backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Match training and generated inference, reading the final real position."
}
